    "body": "Backed by Novartis’ venture arm, El Lilly and CRISPR Therapeutics, Seawolf is advancing its nonviral DNA delivery platform while inking a pact with Sanofi.\nSeawolf Therapeutics is unleashing with $100 million, while also spearing an up to $1.2 billion alliance with Sanofi to develop new redosable DNA medicines.\nThe San Diego biotech’s series A included support from founding investor Versant Ventures, plus Novartis Venture Fund (NVF), Eli Lilly, CRISPR Therapeutics and others, according to a Monday news release.\nSeawolf has emerged wielding a Trident—the biotech’s DNA delivery platform. The redosable lipid nanoparticle (LNP)-based system is designed to deliver DNA-based payloads made to last longer than RNA-based LNP therapies while matching their potency, safety and scalability. Animal testing has shown that the nucleic acid-based therapies are potent, durable and have a clean safety profile, according to Seawolf.\nWhile Trident can be deployed across a range of therapeutic areas, Seawolf’s lead program is designed to address metabolic diseases. The company did not share other specifics regarding its lead asset.\nSeawolf has also reeled in a multi-target research and exclusive licensing deal with Sanofi, as disclosed in the same release. The duo will work to develop undisclosed therapies for bleeding disorders and other indications, with Seawolf eligible to receive up to $1.2 billion in biobucks, plus tiered royalties on potential sales related to the alliance.\n“Seawolf is charting a new course in patient care through non-virally delivered DNA,” Seawolf CEO Elizabeth Alcamo said in the release. Alcamo was a former entrepreneur in residence for Versant and held senior roles at CODA Biotherapeutics and 4D Molecular Therapeutics before that.\nEarnings\nSanofi targets late-phase and commercial M&A to offset looming Dupixent decline\nFocused on ensuring growth beyond Dupixent, Sanofi wants to acquire derisked assets that may benefit from the French pharma’s commercial capabilities, executives said on Friday as they reported third-quarter earnings.\nOctober 30, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nNick Paul Taylor\nRead more",
